1 CREATE
    The CREATE command creates a file, as VMS DCL's CREATE does, from
    the lines of SYS$INPUT: at the terminal, the lines typed up to
    Ctrl/Z; in a command procedure, the data lines after the CREATE
    command, up to the next line that starts with "$" (or the end of a
    DECK).  The file is created even when there are no lines.

    Syntax:

        CREATE [/LOG] <file>[/HOST]

    Parameter:

        <file>          The file to create.  A file on a mounted ODS-2
                        volume, such as DUA0:[WORK]NOTES.TXT or, with
                        SET DEFAULT on a volume, NOTES.TXT, is a
                        variable-length record file with carriage-return
                        carriage control, as VMS makes a text file; it
                        is a new version if the file already exists.  A
                        host file makes a plain host text file instead,
                        each line ending as the host's do: LF on Unix
                        and macOS, CR LF on Windows.  A name with a host
                        path ("./notes.txt", quoted to keep its case) is
                        a host file; so is a bare name when the default
                        directory isn't on a mounted volume.

    Qualifiers:

        /HOST           Attached to <file>, makes it a host file name,
                        whatever it looks like: CREATE "notes.txt"/HOST.

        /LOG            Report the file created, with its version.

    Examples:

        CREATE NOTES.TXT
        This is the first line.
        This is the last.
        ^Z

        $ CREATE/LOG DUA0:[WORK]SETUP.DAT
        ITEM ONE
        ITEM TWO
        $ TYPE DUA0:[WORK]SETUP.DAT

    CREATE has other forms, each its own command:

        CREATE/DIRECTORY    creates a directory on a mounted volume.
        CREATE/FDL          creates an empty file from an FDL file.
        CREATE/NAME_TABLE   creates a logical name table.

    For details, see:

        HELP CREATE /DIRECTORY
        HELP CREATE /FDL
        HELP CREATE /NAME_TABLE

    See also the help for TYPE and SET DEFAULT.
2 /DIRECTORY
    The CREATE/DIRECTORY command creates a directory on a mounted ODS-2
    volume, as VMS DCL's CREATE/DIRECTORY does, and every directory above
    it that doesn't exist yet: CREATE/DIRECTORY [A.B.C] makes [A], [A.B],
    and [A.B.C], whichever are missing.  A directory that already exists
    is reported, and isn't an error.

    Syntax:

        CREATE/DIRECTORY [qualifiers] <directory>[,<directory>...]

    Parameter:

        <directory>     A directory specification, such as [A.B] or
                        DUA0:[A.B].  It may be relative to the current
                        default directory ([.SUB], [-.OTHER]; see SET
                        DEFAULT), and its device may be a logical name.
                        It can't name a file, or use wildcards.  With no
                        device and no default device on a mounted volume,
                        the directory is made on the host instead,
                        relative to the host's current directory.

    Qualifiers:

        /OWNER_UIC=<uic>
        /OWNER_UIC=PARENT
                        The new directory's owner: a UIC, [group,member]
                        in octal, or PARENT for the owner of the
                        directory it's made in, which is also the
                        default.

        /VERSION_LIMIT=<n>
                        The default version limit for files created in
                        the new directory; 0 means no limit.  The default
                        is the limit of the directory it's made in.

        /PROTECTION=(<category>:<access>,...)
                        The new directory's protection: for each of
                        SYSTEM, OWNER, GROUP, and WORLD (or S, O, G, W),
                        the access granted, any of R, W, E, and D, or
                        none.  The default is the protection of the
                        directory it's made in, less delete access; a
                        category not given keeps that default.

        /ALLOCATION=<n> The number of blocks to give the directory at
                        once.  The default is 1.

        /LOG            Reports the directory made.  The directories
                        made above it aren't reported.

    A /VERSION_LIMIT out of range is reported and ignored, and the
    directory still made, as VMS does.

    Examples:

        CREATE/DIRECTORY [PROJECTS.TEST]

        CREATE/DIRECTORY/LOG/OWNER_UIC=[200,201]/VERSION_LIMIT=3 [USER]

        CREATE/DIRECTORY/PROTECTION=(S:RWE,O:RWE,G:RE,W) [.SHARED]

    See also the help for DIRECTORY and SET DEFAULT.
2 /FDL
    The CREATE/FDL command creates an empty file from the description in
    an FDL (File Definition Language) file: sequential, relative, or
    indexed, with its record format, allocation, protection, dates, and
    an indexed file's keys and areas.  RMS makes the file, so what RMS
    refuses is reported with RMS's message.

    Syntax:

        CREATE/FDL=<fdl-file> [/LOG] [<file>]

    Parameters:

        <fdl-file>      The FDL file; its default type is .FDL.

        <file>          The file to create.  Without one, the FDL file's
                        FILE NAME names it.  FILE DEFAULT_NAME supplies
                        what the name leaves out.  An existing file gets
                        a new version.

    Qualifiers:

        /LOG            Report the file created, with its version.

    An FDL file holds sections, such as FILE, RECORD, AREA n, and KEY n,
    each followed by its attributes, one to a line or separated by
    semicolons; "!" begins a comment.  Names may be abbreviated.  A warning
    leaves that attribute at its default and the file is still made; an
    error means no file.  Indexed files are Prolog 3 only: PROLOG 1 or 2,
    or a primary key whose segments overlap, is RMS$_PLV.

    Example:

        $ CREATE PARTS.FDL
        FILE
            ORGANIZATION indexed
        RECORD
            SIZE 80
        KEY 0
            SEG0_LENGTH 8
        KEY 1
            SEG0_LENGTH 20
            SEG0_POSITION 8
        $ CREATE/FDL=PARTS/LOG PARTS.IDX
        %FDL-I-CREATED, DUA1:[000000]PARTS.IDX;1 created

    See also the help for OPEN, READ, and WRITE.
2 /NAME_TABLE
    The CREATE/NAME_TABLE command creates a logical name table.

    Syntax:

         CREATE/NAME_TABLE table-name

    Qualifiers:

        /PARENT_TABLE=table

            The new table's parent.  The default, LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY,
            makes a table private to the process.  A shareable parent
            such as LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY makes a shareable table.

        /USER_MODE, /SUPERVISOR_MODE, /EXECUTIVE_MODE

            The access mode of the table.  The default is supervisor mode.

        /LOG (default), /NOLOG

            Report when an existing table is superseded.

    To have file lookups search the new table, add it to LNM$PROCESS:

         $ DEFINE/TABLE=LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY LNM$PROCESS -
                LNM$PROCESS_TABLE, TAX
